Two-bin W_pipr scheme, split at 1.15 GeV/c^2.

WHY. The six-bin equal-population scheme is finer than the detector resolves. Measured
residual RMS 0.142 GeV/c^2, robust half-width 0.101, bias -0.111, against bins as narrow as
0.040 -- two to three times narrower than the resolution, with a bias exceeding three of the
six bin widths. Its migration diagonals are 87.6/22.3/11.7/12.0/10.0/2.3%: one usable bin
of six, the top bin effectively unmeasured. W_pipr is built from the pion and proton momenta
and so inherits the range-momentum saturation; it cannot resolve better than its inputs.

WHY 1.15. An exhaustive edge scan (macros/w_binning_scan.C) finds two bins to be the only
scheme satisfying the >0.68 criterion, and the usable window is narrow:

  boundary  bin1   bin2   N(bin1)
    1.13    76.5%  88.0%     51    below the 100-event floor
    1.15    83.2%  77.9%    137    ADOPTED
    1.17    86.8%  66.8%    266    bin 2 just fails
    1.19    87.6%  56.0%    468    fails

1.15 is the only boundary clearing the criterion in both bins with at least 100 events.
Bin 1 is thin at 137 events and that should be stated wherever the result is quoted; 1.17
doubles the statistics for a bin-2 diagonal of 66.8%, just under threshold, and is the
obvious fallback if the first bin proves too sparse once systematics are folded in.

Three and four bin schemes fail at any edges (best 83.2/49.7/45.9 and 83.2/45.0/28.2/28.2).
